# Attention ordering

Waiting, succeeded, and failed agents have attention; idle, running, and a
manual flag alone do not. Name is an explicit secondary comparator, ordering
the alert group alphabetically rather than by lifecycle severity.

    :b jj tmux cat (available)
    :t rename-session -t 0 runner (success)
    :t new-session -d -s waiting cat (success)
    :t set-option -p -t waiting:0.0 @smth.agent.state waiting (success)
    :t new-session -d -s succeeded cat (success)
    :t set-option -p -t succeeded:0.0 @smth.agent.state succeeded (success)
    :t new-session -d -s failed cat (success)
    :t set-option -p -t failed:0.0 @smth.agent.state failed (success)
    :t new-session -d -s running cat (success)
    :t set-option -p -t running:0.0 @smth.agent.state running (success)
    :t new-session -d -s idle cat (success)
    :t set-option -p -t idle:0.0 @smth.agent.state idle (success)
    :t set-option -t idle @smth.flag 1 (success)
    :$ smth --filter --order '-attn +name' (exit: 0)

```stdout
failed
succeeded
waiting
idle
runner
running
```

Reverse attention to put non-alert sessions first. The explicit name comparator
continues to order equal alert states ascending.

    :$ smth --filter --order '+attn +name' (exit: 0)

```stdout
idle
runner
running
failed
succeeded
waiting
```

    :t resize-window -t runner:0 -x 120 -y 22 (success)
    :t respawn-pane -k -t runner:0 'smth --order "-attn +name"; cat' (success)
    :p runner:0.0
    :settle -d 2s (settled)
    :k C-p
